    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize if I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the science.\n\nThe crystals that form in chocolate are called \"beta crystals,\" and they're formed when the cocoa butter molecules in the chocolate cool and solidify too quickly. This can happen when the chocolate is heated too quickly or cooled too slowly.\n\nTo prevent beta crystals from forming, you want to aim for a temperature range between 82°F and 90°F (28°C and 32°C). Here's a general outline of the tempering process:\n\n1. Melt the chocolate to around 105°F (40°C) to dissolve the cocoa butter.\n2. Cool the chocolate to around 82°F (28°C) to start the crystallization process.\n3. Hold the temperature at around 82°F (28°C) for about 10-15 minutes to allow the crystals to form.\n4. Heat the chocolate to around 88°F (31°C) to \"seed\" the crystals and create a stable structure.\n5. Cool the chocolate to around 82°F (28°C) again to set the crystals and achieve a stable temper.\n\nIt's worth noting that the exact temperature stages may vary depending on the type of chocolate and",
      "source": "target",
